  ],
  "history": "Dog is one of the great mysteries of English. In Old English it appears only once, as docga, in a word list from around 1050. The normal word for the animal was hund. Nobody knows where docga came from; it has no clear relatives in other languages. It may first have meant a large, strong kind of dog. Over the centuries dog became the everyday word, and hound was left for dogs that hunt. Hound is related to German Hund and Dutch hond, and, much further back, to Latin canis and Greek kýōn. Later, dog went abroad: German Dogge, a large breed, was borrowed from English.",
  "historyEinfach": "Dog is a mystery word. We don't know where it comes from. A thousand years ago, the English word for dog was hund. Today, hound is a dog for hunting. In German, every dog is a Hund.",
